  "segments": [
    {
      "text": "Why can you see through a microwave door if the microwaves are supposed to stay",
      "start": 0.106,
      "end": 3.589
    },
    {
      "text": "trapped inside?",
      "start": 3.589,
      "end": 4.411
    },
    {
      "text": "Because that black metal mesh is doing something clever.",
      "start": 4.68,
      "end": 7.18
    },
    {
      "text": "Microwave energy has a wavelength much larger than those tiny openings, so the",
      "start": 7.549,
      "end": 11.768
    },
    {
      "text": "conductive screen strongly blocks it from escaping.",
      "start": 11.768,
      "end": 14.321
    },
    {
      "text": "Visible light has a vastly shorter wavelength, so light can pass through the",
      "start": 14.652,
      "end": 18.432
    },
    {
      "text": "holes and reach your eyes,",
      "start": 18.432,
      "end": 19.771
    },
    {
      "text": "which means the same door can behave like a window for you while acting like a",
      "start": 20.058,
      "end": 23.737
    },
    {
      "text": "barrier for the energy cooking your leftovers.",
      "start": 23.737,
      "end": 25.917
    },
    {
      "text": "Your microwave is basically wearing extremely nerdy sunglasses.",
      "start": 26.243,
      "end": 29.457
    }
  ],
